From d4ad36b1bd43cf91d08c011297e0aaba0a816e32 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:03:05 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Take onion messages from peers without channels, and
 reply where the request went

The onion messages a node on this chain exists to receive, a pool's
invoice requests for its offers and the replies to its own requests,
come from nodes that have no channel with it, and Core Lightning hands
an onion message straight to any node it is connected to, channel or
not. lnd's channel-presence gate dropped them before reading them: in
the lab, a reply from Core Lightning relayed through a peer of the
requester was lost at that peer. The gate is now off by default;
protocol.onion-msg-channel-gate turns it back on, and the rate limiters
still bound what any peer can send. Since the gate no longer keeps
strangers out of the per-peer limiter registry, the registry is bounded:
past 4096 buckets a newcomer takes the place of a bucket that has
refilled, which changes nothing for that peer, or shares one overflow
bucket with every other newcomer until one frees up.

A request that goes straight to the node that reads it, as a peer or
over a one-hop path whose introduction node is that peer, now carries a
reply path that starts at this node: that node sees the connection the
request arrives on anyway, and a path through a peer of ours only adds a
hop whose owner may drop messages from strangers. A request sent any
other way keeps a reply path that starts at a peer, so the recipient
learns a peer of ours and not us.

The Core Lightning port of this chain (privkeyio/lightning) keeps
Bitcoin's chain identity, so the two nodes refuse each other at init;
contrib/cln-chain-identity holds a patch series giving it the identity
in docs/blake2b-chain-identity.md, with the proposal and the lab
results. The document now says in which byte order the tagged hash is
computed, which it did not.

(cherry picked from commit 2dabebd7e95621add42317813da2df8cf323ad51)
